  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\n\nThis approach uses a genetic algorithm (GA) with a period-based representation to minimize the maximum overlap integral for the Erds minimum overlap problem. Unlike previous gradient-based methods, this approach leverages the insight that optimal solutions often exhibit underlying periodic structure. The core idea is to:\n\n1. Represent the function as a periodic signal with a fixed period T (a divisor of 560 samples)\n2. Optimize the base block of length T using genetic programming to minimize the overlap integral\n3. Expand the base block to the full 560-point solution by periodic repetition\n\nThe central difference is using GA to explicitly search for optimal base blocks rather than relying on gradient information. This should help escape local optima by exploring diverse periodic patterns across different scales. The representation leverages the high-dimensional space's implicit structure, improving optimization efficiency.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n\n- Representation: A periodic function h(x) = \u2211_{k=1}^m a_k(x) where each a_k(x) is an atomic block of period T_k (T_k divides 560) that provides a basic pattern in [0,1].\n- Variables: Quantized coefficient vector c (real) of length n_period, with period length T, must satisfy:\n  0 \u2264 c[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  \u2211_{i} \u2211_{j} c[i] * prod_terms[i,j] = 1 (integral constraint)\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = dx * max_lag np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n- Constraints: Boundary conditions h[0]=h[560-1]=0.5, Fix integral by repair function\n- Repair: Use gradient repair (active pattern selection) followed by clipping to enforce bounds and integral\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with periodic patterns (cos(2\u03c0x/a) for a \u2208 {560/m for m|560})\n   - Repair to satisfy bounds [0,1] and integral constraint\n\n2. Genetic Algorithm:\n\n   a. Chromosome Encoding (length T):\n      - Individual: real vector of length T (base block) initially uniform\n      - Repair: Clip to [0,1] and adjust sum by uniform redistriution\n      - Fitness: -1 * overlap_score(base_block)\n\n   b. Genetic Operators:\n      - Selection: Tournament selection (size=3)\n      - Crossover: Simulated binary crossover\n      - Mutation: Polynomial mutation (eta=20)\n\n   c. Period Analysis:\n      - Use divisors of 560: [1,2,4,5,8,...,560]\n      - Start with small divisors for efficiency\n\n   d. Implementation:\n      for T in sorted_divisors:\n        population = [repair(uniform(0,1,T)) for _ in range(50)]\n        while timeout not reached:\n          eval_fitness(population)\n          select parents\n          create_new_generation\n        best_solution = best_chromosome\n\n   e. Final Output:\n        Run best period analysis\n        Repeat with local mutation\n        Return best solution overall\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n\n1. Default: Simulated binary crossover, polynomial mutation, tournament selection\n2. Active Pattern: When fitness plateaus, use gradient-directed mutation on high-gradient indices\n3. Local Search: After main GA, apply Nelder-Mead local search for refinement\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n\n1. Dominant Time: GA runs for all divisors (12 divisors) with 50 individuals\n2. Time Estimation:\n   - Small period T=1: 10 sec\n   - Medium period T=4: 40 sec\n   - Total ~20000 operations\n3. Timeout Handling:\n   - Each period: 90 sec\n   - Repair any outdated chromosomes\n   - Use guided mutation for problematic areas\n\nValidation and return contract:\n\n1. Output Format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points=len(best_solution))\n2. Repair: After every operation, ensure sum(h)=1.28 and bounds [0,1]\n3. Evaluation: Always compute C5 using np.correlate with mode=\"full\" and *dx",
